1
00:00:04,260 --> 00:00:10,050
This is you. Every time you get paid,
or pay someone, you leave a trace.

2
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:14,470
This video is about one question:
who gets to read it?

3
00:00:16,170 --> 00:00:19,560
Step one: Bitcoin, 2009.

4
00:00:20,110 --> 00:00:23,110
For the first time,
two strangers could send money

5
00:00:23,150 --> 00:00:25,860
over the internet with
no bank in the middle.

6
00:00:26,460 --> 00:00:28,820
Instead of a bank's private records,

7
00:00:28,860 --> 00:00:34,290
everyone keeps a copy of one shared
notebook: who paid whom, and how much.

8
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:36,990
But that notebook is public.

9
00:00:37,080 --> 00:00:39,520
Picture your bank pinning
your monthly statement

10
00:00:39,560 --> 00:00:43,010
to a board outside,
for anyone walking past.

11
00:00:43,180 --> 00:00:45,140
Dr. Daniel Kim put it plainly:

12
00:00:45,180 --> 00:00:50,170
everybody in the world can see
who sent what to whom and when.

13
00:00:51,760 --> 00:00:55,830
Early explainers called
Bitcoin anonymous. It isn't.

14
00:00:55,960 --> 00:00:57,760
Your name isn't on the board,

15
00:00:57,800 --> 00:01:02,130
but one payment tied to your name
can be enough to unravel the rest.

16
00:01:03,050 --> 00:01:06,810
Bitcoin also kept its built-in
programming language small,

17
00:01:06,850 --> 00:01:10,700
on purpose: sturdy,
but hard to build apps on.

18
00:01:11,190 --> 00:01:15,740
So step one gave us money with
no bank, and a perfect memory.

19
00:01:18,140 --> 00:01:21,550
Step two: Ethereum, 2015.

20
00:01:21,900 --> 00:01:25,410
What if the shared notebook
could also run programs?

21
00:01:25,580 --> 00:01:30,210
Put money in, the rules run on their
own, and no clerk can change them.

22
00:01:30,820 --> 00:01:35,530
People built exchanges, loans,
games and savings apps on top.

23
00:01:35,780 --> 00:01:38,790
Ethereum proved money can be programmed.

24
00:01:39,480 --> 00:01:42,650
But those programs run
on the same public board.

25
00:01:42,940 --> 00:01:45,360
Now it doesn't just show what you paid.

26
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:49,030
It shows which apps you use,
and everyone you deal with.

27
00:01:51,050 --> 00:01:52,960
Here's where it gets personal.

28
00:01:53,150 --> 00:01:54,820
Say you sell t-shirts.

29
00:01:54,910 --> 00:01:56,440
A customer pays you.

30
00:01:56,690 --> 00:01:58,370
You don't know that months ago,

31
00:01:58,410 --> 00:02:01,700
their coins passed near
a wallet that someone flagged.

32
00:02:01,970 --> 00:02:05,100
Software that watches the
board connects the dots.

33
00:02:06,700 --> 00:02:09,390
To the software, you're one hop away.

34
00:02:09,560 --> 00:02:11,650
That's a guess, not proof.

35
00:02:11,860 --> 00:02:17,510
But you find out afterwards: your account
is closed, your bank freezes your funds.

36
00:02:17,660 --> 00:02:21,470
It had nothing to do with anything
that the person in question did.

37
00:02:22,360 --> 00:02:24,930
And it's not just
payments that get scored.

38
00:02:25,120 --> 00:02:28,120
Banks are told to rate
every customer for risk,

39
00:02:28,160 --> 00:02:31,230
and your passport's country
is part of that score.

40
00:02:31,500 --> 00:02:35,070
Sometimes even where you were
born, which you never chose.

41
00:02:35,280 --> 00:02:38,350
When everything is watched,
the default flips:

42
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:41,190
you're guilty until you
prove you're innocent.

43
00:02:46,020 --> 00:02:50,310
Now rewind to 2014,
a year before Ethereum.

44
00:02:50,600 --> 00:02:53,490
Monero took a different
road out of Bitcoin:

45
00:02:53,760 --> 00:02:57,970
make the money itself private,
for everyone, by default.

46
00:02:58,770 --> 00:03:02,410
When you pay, your payment
hides in a crowd of decoys,

47
00:03:02,450 --> 00:03:04,880
so nobody can tell which one is you.

48
00:03:05,030 --> 00:03:06,580
The amount is hidden.

49
00:03:06,770 --> 00:03:10,380
And the person you pay gets
a fresh, one-time address.

50
00:03:11,380 --> 00:03:15,370
Picture a banknote with every
past owner listed on the back.

51
00:03:15,560 --> 00:03:19,390
Monero wipes the list:
money with no memory.

52
00:03:20,590 --> 00:03:23,940
Isn't privacy just for criminals? No.

53
00:03:24,090 --> 00:03:29,080
Privacy protects the shop owner,
the nurse, the person sending money home.

54
00:03:30,100 --> 00:03:34,930
But Monero is built for one job:
private money, not apps.

55
00:03:35,160 --> 00:03:39,590
So now there are two roads:
one programmable, one private.

56
00:03:41,260 --> 00:03:46,170
Step four is where the roads meet:
programs that run on a private network.

57
00:03:46,300 --> 00:03:47,510
That's Tari.

58
00:03:50,100 --> 00:03:51,970
Tari has two layers.

59
00:03:52,060 --> 00:03:55,230
The base layer holds a coin called XTM.

60
00:03:55,440 --> 00:03:58,220
Miners keep it safe, and Monero miners can

61
00:03:58,260 --> 00:04:01,330
secure it at the same time,
with the same work.

62
00:04:02,130 --> 00:04:05,720
On top sits the Ootle,
Tari's layer for apps.

63
00:04:06,850 --> 00:04:11,880
The Ootle's own coin, also
called TARI, is hidden by design:

64
00:04:12,170 --> 00:04:14,990
no one else can see the
amount or the owner,

65
00:04:15,030 --> 00:04:18,200
and every payment lands
at a one-time address.

66
00:04:18,450 --> 00:04:22,230
The network can still check that
nobody made money out of thin air,

67
00:04:22,270 --> 00:04:23,960
without seeing the numbers.

68
00:04:24,970 --> 00:04:28,020
Builders choose how private each asset is.

69
00:04:28,270 --> 00:04:32,360
And a view key is a read-only key
you can hand to an accountant:

70
00:04:32,490 --> 00:04:34,940
they can see, but they can't spend.

71
00:04:35,710 --> 00:04:39,770
To move in, you burn XTM
on the base layer and claim

72
00:04:39,810 --> 00:04:43,840
the same amount of TARI on the
Ootle, minus a small fee.

73
00:04:44,110 --> 00:04:45,500
A one-way door.

74
00:04:46,310 --> 00:04:50,650
An app on the Ootle runs by its
own rules, like on Ethereum,

75
00:04:50,690 --> 00:04:54,320
and the money it moves can
stay hidden, like on Monero.

76
00:04:54,610 --> 00:04:56,810
Teams of computers check the work,

77
00:04:56,850 --> 00:05:00,320
and each team looks after
its own piece of the records.

78
00:05:00,630 --> 00:05:04,900
Anyone can see that money moved,
but not how much, or whose.

79
00:05:05,700 --> 00:05:07,010
It's not magic.

80
00:05:07,080 --> 00:05:11,650
Fees are public, and anything
a builder makes public stays public.

81
00:05:11,800 --> 00:05:16,130
Privacy is a choice, and Tari
builds it into the foundations.

82
00:05:17,830 --> 00:05:19,140
Remember the flag?

83
00:05:19,310 --> 00:05:23,040
It worked because a stranger's
software could read your history.

84
00:05:23,250 --> 00:05:26,360
On a private network,
there's no board to read.

85
00:05:26,410 --> 00:05:31,380
The network itself doesn't ask for your
passport, and no one has to approve you.

86
00:05:31,490 --> 00:05:33,580
Your business stays yours.

87
00:05:34,600 --> 00:05:36,570
Step four starts now.
